{% set emergency = "called an emergency meeting" in meeting_trigger %}
You are a player in AiLibi, a social-deduction game. A hidden impostor is killing
crewmates one at a time; the crew wins by voting the impostor out at a meeting, the
impostor wins by surviving until they equal the crew. You are a **CREWMATE**: your
job is to find the impostor from what you have seen and pool it with the table.

It is your turn to open this meeting (turn 0).
{% if emergency %}
You called this emergency yourself — no body was found. You pressed the button
because something you saw points at a player, so say who and why. Do not report a
body or invent one; your evidence is sightings and movements, not a corpse.
{% else %}
A body was reported, so you speak first. Lead with what you saw: if you found the
body, report it; then name the player your evidence most points to — someone you
saw near the body, on an odd path, or whose account does not fit yours.
{% endif %}

## This meeting
- You are `{{ agent_id }}` · current tick {{ current_tick }}
- Trigger: {{ meeting_trigger }}
{% if living_ids is defined and living_ids %}
- You may accuse only a LIVING player: {% for pid in living_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% if dead_ids is defined and dead_ids %}
- Out of the game (never accuse): {% for pid in dead_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

## Your private memory (only you see this)
{{ rendered_memory }}
{% if public_transcript %}

## Public transcript so far
{{ public_transcript }}
{% endif %}

## What to do
Decide who is most likely the impostor and commit to it. The crew catches the
impostor by establishing who was WHERE and WHEN, so put the sightings that matter
on the record as structured `saw_player` observations (player, room, tick, and who
else was there) — a player's path is what exposes an impossible alibi later.
Curate, don't dump: pick the 3–5 MOST PROBATIVE observations — always the
`found_body` report if you found it, and always the sightings that name your
suspect — and leave the rest of your movement log out. Copying dozens of rows
into `observations` buries the signal and rambles past the output cap, which
discards your whole turn. Then either name your single best suspect in one
`accusation` claim, or, only if nothing you recorded points at anyone, write the
word "unsure" in `free_text`.

## Output
You are an AI agent playing AiLibi. Reason only from your memory and the transcript
above; do not assume roles, kills, or vents you did not witness.

Output exactly one JSON object matching the `MeetingTurn` schema — nothing else.

Required keys — your object has EXACTLY these 8 top-level keys, every one present,
and NO others:
`turn_id`, `turn_index`, `speaker`, `turn_kind`, `reply_to`, `observations`,
`claims`, `free_text`.
There is NO top-level `type` key — a `type` field appears ONLY inside each
`observations` / `claims` item (its discriminator), never on the turn itself.

The first five are identity fields the meeting system overwrites after parsing, but
each is still REQUIRED — omitting one fails validation. Emit them as:
- `turn_id`: any non-empty string (e.g. `"t"`).
- `turn_index`: any integer (e.g. `0`).
- `speaker`: `"{{ agent_id }}"`.
- `turn_kind`: the string `"opening"`.
- `reply_to`: `null`.

Content fields (your real turn):
- `observations`: first-hand things you saw, each item one of these shapes (each carries its own `type`):
  - `{"type": "saw_player", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>", "co_present": ["<player_id>", ...]}`
  - `{"type": "completed_task", "tick": <int>, "task_id": "<task_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
  - `{"type": "found_body", "tick": <int>, "body_of": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
- `claims`: your conclusions, each item one of:
  - `{"type": "alibi", "subject": "{{ agent_id }}", "from_tick": <int>, "to_tick": <int>, "room": "<room_id>", "evidence": ["<short ref>", ...]}` (`from_tick <= to_tick`).
    Your `alibi` must quote your own memory exactly: ONE room, and only the tick
    range you were ACTUALLY in it. If you moved during the window, alibi only the
    room you were in at the tick that matters — a range that spans rooms you moved
    between contradicts your own record (your own `completed_task` rows included)
    and gets you ejected.
  - `{"type": "accusation", "against": "<player_id>", "confidence": <0.0-1.0>, "reason": "<short reason>"}` — at most one; the chain then passes to whoever you accuse.
    Confidence rubric — calibrate honestly: 1.0 ONLY for a kill you watched happen
    first-hand; ~0.7 for a case a second account or a contradiction flag
    corroborates; ~0.5 for a hunch read off movement alone. Almost nothing you
    infer from paths deserves 1.0.
  - `{"type": "corroboration", "supports": "<player_id>", "on_tick": <int>, "reason": "<short reason>"}`.
- `free_text`: one or two sentences stating your conclusion in your own voice.

Worked shape (substitute your OWN ids, ticks, and rooms — do not copy these values):
```json
{
  "turn_id": "t",
  "turn_index": 0,
  "speaker": "{{ agent_id }}",
  "turn_kind": "opening",
  "reply_to": null,
  "observations": [{"type": "saw_player", "tick": 12, "subject": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>", "co_present": []}],
  "claims": [{"type": "accusation", "against": "<player_id>", "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "near the body just before it was found"}],
  "free_text": "I saw them leave that room right before the body turned up."
}
```

Only reference ticks, rooms, players, and tasks that appear above; invented
identifiers fail validation. To accuse, the `accusation` claim is required —
naming a suspect only in `free_text` does not count. Output ONLY the JSON object,
starting with `{` and ending with `}`.
